sses and the opportunity  that's there as well the competition has  really resulted in a benefit for  consumers and businesses across the land  that will then benefit for  the knock-on and cheaper energy costs  going through i think  where there is more challenge and where  there's um clearly processes on going to  science around  investing in the infrastructure and the  network that will connect all that green  generation  that's more of a challenge right now um  and clearly there's there's  a process is ongoing i think there's  a need to ensure that the regulatory  framework keeps a pace  with the aspiration with regard to green  recovery and net zero  and again we shouldn't be looking  backwards this is we have to be more  progressive we have to be  forward-looking  we have to ensure that for example the  right  mechanisms are there to ensure that when  somebody does go out and purchase their  first electric vehicle or indeed hire an  electric vehicle  that they have got ready access to a  charge point in the vicinity of where  they live or where they intend to use  that vehicle  and indeed when you're starting to look  at heavy haulage or  a larger larger scale transportation or  transportation into more rural  as a country that they will have access  to the necessary hydrogen equipment that  they need  to make sure that their fleet can run  and we need to make sure that all of  that is there  in advance there's a bit of um future  proofing requirement got to be going on  and and we have to go with those again  those technologies that we know when are  proven so  my view is we almost have the toolkit  that we need  we just need to make sure that we're  using it properly  the opportunity is there an investment  will come  i think the challenges are around making  sure that we don't see that investment  trickle off into other international  markets  the uk currently does have a lead  position when it comes to lead  investing in green energy we should all  want to 