 time it was seen as preposterous  that any of that would deliver  and i think at the outset of some of  these policies they will look very very  ambitious and stretching and challenging  and people will naturally be skeptical  that the government can deliver it  or will have enough charge points all  these details  and i think that will be really where  our our challenge is  to make sure that we've got a  coordinated plan that we can present to  the public  um to answer all those sort of common  sense questions i mean what you know  when i go out into my constituency  at the weekend obviously in a socially  distanced way  um for an essential reason that those  are the sorts of things that people will  say to me well  you know i can't afford an electric car  or i haven't got a charger or what about  when i drive to scotland  i think those will be the the  nitty-gritty of it  and that's obviously what we need to do  as ministers i think that will be  what we'll really will have to wrestle  with  uh will what do you think the biggest  barrier is that  i'm i'm greatly in agreement with what  rachel just said public attitude and  actually  the shift from the current ways of  travel  um to the future ways to travel  you know there's going to be i think  huge resistance to the withdrawal of  petrol and diesel combustion engines  in 10 years or less than 10 years time  2030 but that again is  another catalyst will move in um but i  think that  the other barriers what i've mentioned  already it's about  having the right framework so that you  know  we as private industry uh whether it's  you know  isabelle side of things or my side of  things or indeed you know  wherever it is have the right mechanism  in order to be able to invest to move  things forwards  take down the barriers of bureaucracy  that we sometimes still see in trying to  introduce new technologies  and move forward and i think we can  learn so much from what's happened  in um the power generation market and 